On the topic of growing older in the public eye, Eilish began by laughing at her younger self for previously thinking that she&#8217;d stay the same for the rest of her life. &#8220;I never thought I would not be a teenager,&#8221; she told Poehler. &#8220;I remember when I was 17, I was like, &#8216;OK, I am the person I&#8217;ll be forever right now.&#8217; And that&#8217;s not how it works, obviously.&#8221;

Now, the nine-time Grammy winner doesn&#8217;t just embrace change, both physical and personal &#8212; she&#8217;s also eager to see it in action. &#8220;I am so excited to age, and I&#8217;m so excited for my face to age and my body to age and not change it,&#8221; Eilish continued. &#8220;I want my kids to look at me and have my face look like their face and not be some botched version of whatever the f&#8211;k is going on out there right now.&#8221;

The podcast appearance comes as the singer is gearing up to release her 3D Hit Me Hard and Soft concert film, which she co-directed alongside James Cameron, on May 8. It&#8217;s not the first time she&#8217;s addressed the topic of cosmetic procedures, having told The Guardian in 2021, &#8220;It’s completely fine to get work done &#8212; do this, do that, do what makes you feel happy.&#8221;

&#8220;It’s just when you deny it and say, ‘Oh, I got this all on my own, and if you just tried harder, you could get it,'&#8221; she continued at the time. &#8220;That makes me literally furious. It is so bad for young women &#8212; and boys, too &#8212; to see that.&#8221;

Elsewhere on Good Hang , Eilish spoke about another type of authenticity she values. Known for being blunt and sometimes brash in interviews &#8212; particularly when she first found fame as a young teenager &#8212; the two-time Oscar winner revealed that she once did a single session of PR training when she was 14 and hated every minute of it.

&#8220;I sobbed through it,&#8221; she recalled. &#8220;I hated it so much. It was literally the scariest s&#8211;t of all time. I only did one session and it was under an hour, and I sobbed, and I left, and I did not follow any of the rules after that.&#8221;

